1
00:00:00,135 --> 00:00:01,480
See this glowing dot?

2
00:00:01,480 --> 00:00:02,910
That's the whole universe.

3
00:00:02,955 --> 00:00:03,795
To see why,

4
00:00:03,795 --> 00:00:04,615
look at this.

5
00:00:04,615 --> 00:00:07,095
This is the first real photo of a black hole,

6
00:00:07,095 --> 00:00:08,790
released in 2019.

7
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:10,188
It's as heavy as

8
00:00:10,188 --> 00:00:12,355
six and a half billion Suns.

9
00:00:12,355 --> 00:00:13,400
And our whole solar system

10
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:15,180
is this tiny circle.

11
00:00:15,735 --> 00:00:16,445
And up close,

12
00:00:16,445 --> 00:00:17,935
it would look like this.

13
00:00:17,935 --> 00:00:19,820
The bright ring is hot gas,

14
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:21,009
spinning around it.

15
00:00:21,255 --> 00:00:22,659
Let's look at it from above.

16
00:00:23,535 --> 00:00:24,290
See?

17
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:25,967
The disk is actually flat.

18
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:27,775
This is the back half.

19
00:00:27,775 --> 00:00:29,507
And this is the front half.

20
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:30,700
As we go back down,

21
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:31,600
you'd expect it to hide

22
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:33,247
behind the black hole.

23
00:00:33,405 --> 00:00:35,285
But gravity bends its light,

24
00:00:35,285 --> 00:00:36,615
up over the top...

25
00:00:36,615 --> 00:00:38,247
and under the bottom.

26
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:39,655
In 1979,

27
00:00:39,655 --> 00:00:41,970
Jean-Pierre Luminet drew this by hand,

28
00:00:41,970 --> 00:00:43,335
dot by dot.

29
00:00:43,335 --> 00:00:44,540
And Interstellar's black hole

30
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:46,820
used the same physics.

31
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:48,650
Now let's fly in closer.

32
00:00:48,650 --> 00:00:49,927
Much closer.

33
00:00:50,365 --> 00:00:51,820
If you hover right here,

34
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:52,760
the black hole fills

35
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:54,819
exactly half your sky.

36
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:56,635
And see this thin line?

37
00:00:56,635 --> 00:00:57,145
It's light that goes

38
00:00:57,145 --> 00:00:59,785
all the way around the black hole...

39
00:00:59,785 --> 00:01:00,959
and comes back to you.

40
00:01:01,290 --> 00:01:02,425
So in this line,

41
00:01:02,425 --> 00:01:04,569
you see the back of your own head.

42
00:01:04,805 --> 00:01:06,139
Now let's go lower,

43
00:01:06,139 --> 00:01:07,960
and hover just above the edge.

44
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:09,140
Then look up.

45
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:10,280
The whole universe shrinks

46
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:13,089
into one small dot above you.

47
00:01:13,505 --> 00:01:14,550
And down here,

48
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:15,809
time runs slower.

49
00:01:16,075 --> 00:01:17,365
Stay for one minute...

50
00:01:17,365 --> 00:01:19,519
and half an hour goes by out there.

51
00:01:19,795 --> 00:01:21,640
Even GPS feels this.

52
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:22,360
Up in orbit,

53
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:23,705
clocks run fast,

54
00:01:23,705 --> 00:01:24,455
and without a fix,

55
00:01:24,455 --> 00:01:25,731
your map would drift

56
00:01:25,731 --> 00:01:27,369
ten kilometers a day.

57
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:29,890
And all this darkness around the dot?

58
00:01:29,890 --> 00:01:31,259
That's the black hole.
